, and the new dispatch-order check is live.\n\n**The retrospective found two faults, both accepted and recorded in `docs/violation-decisions.md`:**\n- **Offline check passed something it hadn't verified (20 min).** The offline test run marked a failing check \"unverified\" and passed it, which cost one LabVIEW run. Decision: the offline run must fail in that case. This is the first thing cycle 134 builds.\n- **Wrong card slot (13 min).** I sent the dispatch-check card in a main slot instead of the separate slot for side work, against my own written decision. That left the cycle one card short.\n\nOn the retrospective's advice, the next act no longer patches the matching code a sixth time. The graph reader will record Flat Sequence frames directly, and session b will be prepared on a's real file.\n\n**Next (cycle 134), from `STATUS.md` NEXT and `tools/bench/next.json`:**\n1. Offline check fix, Flat Sequence frame reader, read a's in-between file, prepare b on it.\n2. Scratch of session b on a copy of that file, with an Error List count between 49 and 52.\n3. One real run of both sessions.\n\nThe steering card from the outcome review was answered \"follow\".\n\n**Question for you (D-2026-10-02-02 in `decisions_pending.json`):** your limit is 6 unrun files in a row (now 3 of 6). Does the in-between file count toward it?\n- I am proceeding as \"no\" (my recommendation): the finished file is identical under any answer, only the count differs.\n- If it counts, the limit would need to go to 8, because P4 and P5 will likely hit the same memory limit.\n\nOne small issue: an older answered item in `decisions_pending.json` (D-2026-09-28-01) is longer than the file checker allows. The checker now rejects the whole file. I left the item untouched, because items are never re-worded.","ttft_ms":2680,"type":"result","duration_ms":6991773,"uuid":"df1ed324-9af1-4a24-988c-9a9fec345f73","ttft_stream_ms":1557,"time_to_request_ms":216,"first_content_frame_ms":1558,"queued_turn_count":0,"